WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:04.050
Not all try block code causes an error.

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:06.480
Hopefully, errors are rare.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.270
We have seen how an except statement runs
code if a try block were to cause a runtime

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:12.270
error.

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:15.660
But what if we want code to run if the try
block can run without error?

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:22.660
What if, for example, someone enters a legitimate
value for a game piece and no errors occur?

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:26.800
For that situation, we want an else statement.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:32.480
Continuing the figurative language example
we started earlier in this section, we successfully

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:34.710
tested the try code.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:39.370
We raised an error if someone entered an invalid
value for the range of figuratives.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:47.880
To continue building this code, I have added
an else block to this 523-else file on lines

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:55.060
7 and 8 and moved the print statement to confirm
the choice made to that block.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:58.570
Else runs if the try is successful.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:04.110
While else is not required, we have seen that
although the else block is optional, including

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.979
it can be useful.

00:01:05.979 --> 00:01:10.430
Without it, the code in the try block would
execute if no exceptions occur, but we would

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:17.450
have no way to easily specify additional actions
to take specifically when the try block is

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:18.450
successful.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:24.240
I will run this code and enter a number from
1 through 5.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.549
The code works, and we get a matching figurative.

00:01:29.549 --> 00:01:42.909
I will run the code again and enter a bad
value, and, as expected, we get the exception

00:01:42.909 --> 00:01:44.920
message.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:50.090
To recap what we have learned so far in this
section: a try block tries to run code without

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:51.090
an error.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:54.520
If an error occurs, the except block runs.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:02:00.010
If no errors occur, the else block runs.

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:02.740
We need an except block for an else block
to work.

